    <h2>1. The Mechanical Physics: Why Exactly 2 PSI Below Cut-In?</h2>
    <p>
      Consider what happens as water is consumed inside your home:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>As water exits the pressure tank, the compressed air cushion expands, driving water out through the manifold while system pressure steadily drops.</li>
      <li>When system pressure reaches the <strong>cut-in threshold</strong> of the electromechanical pressure switch (typically 40 PSI), the switch contact points snap closed, energizing the 230V submersible well pump hundreds of feet down in the bedrock borehole.</li>
      <li>It requires roughly 1 to 2 seconds for the electric motor to overcome rotor inertia, spin up to 3,450 RPM, and push water up the long vertical drop pipe to reach the surface.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If your pre-charge air pressure is set at or above the cut-in pressure (e.g., 40 or 42 PSI): the air cushion expands fully and pushes the rubber diaphragm all the way against the bottom steel tank spud <em>before</em> the pump can start supplying fresh water. At 40 PSI, the tank suddenly runs completely out of water. System pressure drops instantly from 40 PSI to 0 PSI! Household water flow stalls, faucets sputter with a violent thud, and the pump is forced to start under dead-head conditions.
    </p>
    <p>
      By calibrating the air cushion to <strong>38 PSI</strong> (2 PSI below the 40 PSI cut-in), there is still approximately 0.5 to 1.0 gallon of water remaining inside the tank when the switch contacts close. That reserve water maintains steady household line pressure during the 2-second interval it takes for the well pump to bring fresh groundwater to the surface.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Boyle's Law &amp; Usable Drawdown Volume Table</h2>
    <p>
      Many homeowners believe that a 32-gallon pressure tank stores 32 gallons of water. This is false. Under <strong>Boyle's Ideal Gas Law</strong> (P1 x V1 = P2 x V2), the air volume inside the captive tank expands and contracts as water enters and leaves. The actual usable water discharged between pump shut-off (cut-out) and pump start (cut-in) is called <strong>drawdown volume</strong>, which averages only 30% of total tank capacity:
    </p>
