    <h2>3. The 4-Step Procedure to Accurately Calibrate Pre-Charge Pressure</h2>
    <p>
      Never attempt to calibrate air pressure on a live, pressurized plumbing system. Follow this exact technical sequence:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>De-Energize Power:</strong> Turn off the dedicated 2-pole 230V circuit breaker supplying the well pump at your electrical panel.</li>
      <li><strong>Drain Water to Zero:</strong> Connect a hose to the tank drain valve and open it fully. Open the highest hot and cold faucets in the house to break vacuum. Continue draining until water stops trickling out and the manifold gauge rests at exactly <strong>0 PSI</strong>. Keep the drain valve open!</li>
      <li><strong>Measure with a Digital Gauge:</strong> Remove the plastic dust cap from the Schrader valve on the top dome of the tank. Press a calibrated digital tire gauge onto the stem.</li>
      <li><strong>Adjust Air Pressure:</strong> If pressure is low, use a manual bicycle pump or portable tire inflator to pump air into the chamber. If pressure is too high, depress the center valve pin to bleed air. Set the reading to precisely 38 PSI (for a 40/60 system) or 28 PSI (for a 30/50 system).</li>
      <li><strong>Close Drain and Re-Energize:</strong> Disconnect the drain hose, close the drain valve, and flip the breaker on. Watch the pressure switch cut in and out cleanly.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>4. Diagnosing a Ruptured Waterlogged Bladder: The 3 Warning Signs</h2>
    <p>
      Captive air tanks eventually fail when the flexible butyl rubber membrane fatigues, tears, or separates from the steel shell. Watch for these three diagnostic signatures:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rapid Pump Short-Cycling:</strong> The well pump clicks on instantly every time a toilet flushes or a faucet runs for 5 seconds, and shuts off 4 seconds later. Because water cannot compress, line pressure spikes and plunges violently without an air buffer.</li>
      <li><strong>Water Ejecting from the Schrader Valve:</strong> When you depress the top air valve pin, water squirts out instead of dry air. This is 100% proof that the internal bladder is torn and water has flooded the air chamber. The tank is ruined and must be replaced.</li>
      <li><strong>The Heavy "Dull Thud" Knock Test:</strong> Rap your knuckles against the tank shell from top to bottom. A healthy tank sounds hollow and resonant in the top 2/3 (air chamber) and solid in the bottom 1/3 (water). A waterlogged tank sounds dead, solid, and heavy all the way to the top.</li>
    </ul>
