    note="A service call I receive constantly is from homeowners who notice their pump clicking repeatedly, go down to the basement, depress the top air valve stem with a screwdriver, and get sprayed in the face with rusty water. When water comes out of the air valve, there is zero question: the internal rubber diaphragm has ripped wide open. What many homeowners do not realize is that running a well system with a waterlogged tank will destroy their deep-well pump motor within days. When the tank is full of water, there is zero air cushion. The pump is forced to start and stop up to 300 times per day. Every startup draws heavy inrush locked-rotor amperage (LRA) across the motor windings, heating the internal stator insulation until it melts and shorts out. A new pressure tank costs $350. A new deep-well submersible pump pulled and replaced by a drilling rig costs $3,500. If water sprays from that air valve, shut the pump breaker off and replace the tank immediately."
